WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:02.390
CARTER MORGAN: We've secured
our dependency chain.

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.790
We've made sure
our code is secure.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:05.690
Add a CAT to that.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.630
It must be secure at
this point, right?

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.440
Wrong.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.510
It's time to look at
protecting our developers

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.060
from devious phishing attacks.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.127
No matter how secure your
code bases from the outside,

00:00:17.127 --> 00:00:19.460
if someone manages to get
into one of your devs' account

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.200
with a good phishing
attack, then all

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.850
of those previous protections?

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.280
Useless.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.080
A typical login flow
looks something like this.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.360
You present your
login to the server.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.290
Then you're in.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.630
But, oh no, someone
was actually giving you

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.580
a very clever phishing
page, and you've

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.105
given them your credentials.

00:00:36.105 --> 00:00:38.480
Now they can do anything you
could have done, like modify

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.230
source code, install malware,
or even send emails with too

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.860
many emojis to your whole team.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.710
What about a one-time password
generated by your phone?

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.130
Unfortunately, that doesn't
always prevent phishing,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.750
because a phisher
can just take that,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.340
pass it forward with
your credentials,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.905
and now they're logging in
as you, just as successfully.

00:00:56.905 --> 00:00:59.280
There has got to be a better
way to secure the people who

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.420
access your system, and that's
where security keys come in.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.459
They can be used as
B keys, or Bluetooth,

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.950
or you can even use your
Android phone as one.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.470
And these serve as a much
stronger second factor

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.720
to make your login secure.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.970
So let's see how
security keys would

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.127
have stopped that previous
phishing attempt in its tracks.

00:01:18.127 --> 00:01:19.710
Instead of being
complete after you've

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.750
given a login name
and a password,

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.730
the server replies
with a challenge,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.100
because it knows that
you registered a security

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.020
key on your account already.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.030
Your security key
accepts that challenge,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.430
verifies that it does
recognize the domain name,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.740
and uses its hidden
private key to sign it.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.147
This means a phishing domain,
even a very cleverly disguised

00:01:38.147 --> 00:01:40.230
one with a URL that looks
almost exactly the same,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.772
except for that
weird ASCII character

00:01:41.772 --> 00:01:44.100
it's using this not really
an O but it looks like an O?

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.710
Yeah, that won't work.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.210
The key won't sign the challenge
and return it to the domain

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.730
if it isn't exactly
what it was set up with.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.980
If everything does check
out, your security key

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.590
accepts the challenge, verifies
that it recognizes the domain

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.320
name, and uses its hidden
private key to sign it.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.570
Finally, the server
uses the public key

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.120
to verify the
signature, and it knows

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.040
you have the same physical
key that you had before.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.430
Now, this is strong
authentication that's

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.860
very resistant to phishing.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.908
Preventing phishing is
just as easy as that.

00:02:13.908 --> 00:02:15.450
Once you set these
keys up and change

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.300
the 2-step authentication
to only use security keys

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.850
in the Google Admin
UI, then the next time

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.490
a developer logs on after
they enter their password,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.740
they'll be asked to
touch their security key.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.080
Plug that in, give it
a tap, now you're in.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.060
That's the end of this
little security journey.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.120
Thank you for going
along this ride with me.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.852
I'm Carter Morgan, and you?

00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:40.910
You are now a certified expert
in all things app security.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.080
Or maybe not.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:43.010
What do you think?

